{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:75de707bd22cc1d0aa1270e056d112ffbf481b21084d63a6c72209d7c3a91d83","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.12,"B":0.56,"C":0.17,"D":0.15}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"LOCK CONFIRMED AND AUDITED. The lexical priority (A if growth>=2.6; else C if core>=2.7 AND growth<2.0; else D if growth<1.7 AND core<2.0; else B) is well-formed and exhaustive, and I apply it exactly. Two audit findings about the lock itself.\nFINDING 1 — WINDOW MISMATCH BETWEEN CQ2 AND THE C TRIGGER. CQ2's inflation conjunct is a 2027-2036 ten-year average; the lock's C trigger is GDP-weighted OECD-38 core over 2032-2036 only. My inflation path is explicitly front-loaded (2027-30 ~2.8% on tariff pass-through, zonal electricity costs, shelter and care scarcity; 2031-36 ~2.35% on capex digestion, J/K/M automation, Chinese overcapacity export deflation and the BIS Box C labour-income demand drag). So my 62% on \"2027-36 core >=2.50%\" implies only ~24% on \"2032-36 core >=2.70%\". A naive reader would map my CQ2 straight onto C and over-weight it by 10-15pp. This is the single most important consistency statement I can make.\nFINDING 2 — THE EM-OECD INFLATION FLOOR SUPPRESSES REGIME D. D requires 2032-36 OECD-38 core BELOW 2.0% on a GDP-weighted basis that INCLUDES Turkey, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica. Those members put a floor of roughly +0.30pp (Turkey alone ~0.25-0.35pp at 2025 PPP weights) under the aggregate, so a sub-2.0% print requires G7 core at ~1.6-1.7% — a deeper Japanification than the narrative \"D\" implies. A naive reader imagining post-bust Japanification assigns D 20-25%; the index construction moves ~5pp of that mass into B. Direction: D down, B up.\nCONSTRUCTION FROM MY OWN CQ3/CQ2.\nA: P(growth >= 2.6) from my CQ3 (P50 1.8, P90 2.7) ≈ 12%. Both legs must beat: OECD-38 ~1.1-1.3 and China ~2.6 produce 1.73; A needs roughly OECD 1.8+ and China 3.5+, i.e. the AI-Q1/AI-Q6 Transformative Breakout tail (16.2% standalone) AND a Chinese consumption rebalancing. 12.\nC: P(growth<2.0) ≈ 0.60 given P50 1.8. P(2032-36 core >= 2.70 | growth<2.0) ≈ 0.28 — above the unconditional 0.24 because the bottleneck channel (grid, construction trades, care wages) is the shared cause of both low growth and high core; capped below by the audited fact that the data-centre electricity shock lands in the ENERGY exclusion, not in core, and that the +635 TWh grid build is a ~20% capex increment rather than a regime-breaking one, so it cannot by itself produce a 2.7% core aggregate. 0.60 x 0.28 = 17%.\nD: P(growth<1.7) ≈ 0.44. P(core<2.0 | growth<1.7) ≈ 0.34 after the EM-OECD floor haircut (would be ~0.42 on a G7-only basket). 0.44 x 0.34 = 15%. No overlap with C (core>=2.7 excluded) or A (growth<1.7).\nB: residual = 100 - 12 - 17 - 15 = 56.\nRESIDUAL-B ABSORPTION QUANTIFIED, as my lens requires. Of the 56, roughly 16pp sits in cells a naive reader would have assigned elsewhere: (i) the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover, growth<1.7 with core 2.0-2.69 — 0.44 x 0.28 ≈ 12pp, which reads as \"stagnation\" and would naively go to D; (ii) growth 2.0-2.5 with core >=2.7 — ~4pp, which reads as \"stagflation\" and would naively go to C. Only ~40pp of my B is the literal \"1.7-2.5% growth barbell\" a reader pictures. So B is favoured by my central path AND inflated by the catch-all; both effects are real and I report them separately rather than netting them.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED: CQ3 P50 = 1.8% < 2.6%, so A is confined to upper-tail mass and is set at 12, not at the regime's narrative appeal; CQ2 = 39 is a JOINT (OECD core >=2.50 over 2027-36 at 62% x China <1.50 at 72% x rho adjustment) and its OECD leg is a different window from the C trigger, so C is built from P(2032-36 core >=2.70 | growth<2.0) = 0.28 and NOT from 0.62; D is built from P(growth<1.7)=0.44 x 0.34; residual B = 56. Sums to exactly 100.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.420Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the 2026 Structure of Earnings Survey microdata with median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, and the 2022-to-2026 change in NACE Q median hourly earnings is NEGATIVE or below the all-sector median change in at least 8 of the 12 largest EU members, despite published collective-agreement wage floors for care staff rising faster than the all-economy average in those members.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029 (SES 2026 reference year, publication typically ~30-36 months after)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS my central CQ1 claim (entrant dilution of the Q median) and would push CQ1 from 6 toward 3-4. The inverse observation — Q median hourly earnings outpacing the all-sector median by >=4pp over 2022-26 in most of those members — falsifies my composition argument and would roughly double CQ1 to 11-13, and would also raise CQ6-C by ~3pp since it implies the care wage channel reaches the core index.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A published OECD or BIS decomposition of G7 core CPI/PCE attributes >=0.40pp/yr of 2027-2029 core inflation to electricity and grid-cost pass-through through non-energy channels (business-cost pass-through into services and processed food), i.e. the data-centre energy shock demonstrably crosses the core exclusion boundary at a scale above my +0.1-0.2pp estimate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 'core excludes the AI energy shock' driver, which is doing most of the work holding CQ6-C down. Would raise CQ6-C from 17 to ~26 (taken from B), raise the OECD leg of CQ2 from 62% to ~72% and CQ2 from 39 to ~45, and raise the C-consistent part of CQ4's distribution (P50 2.0 -> ~2.2).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS publishes a rebased CPI basket (2026 base) under which 2027 and 2028 annual headline CPI both print >=1.8%, with the increase traceable to services and rent weights rather than to a food/pork cycle (i.e. core-equivalent services CPI >=2.0%), and household consumption rises above 42% of GDP in the same national accounts vintage.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my treatment of Chinese CPI as structurally floored near zero by the property adjustment, the low household income share and supply-side industrial policy. Drops the China conjunct of CQ2 from 72% to ~45% and CQ2 from 39 to ~26. Also raises my CQ3 China leg by ~0.4pp (P50 1.8 -> ~2.0) and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-D toward B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >=20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >=100bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — i.e. the classic flight-to-quality reasserts itself despite the 2A plumbing (convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption, >100% debt/GDP).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind CQ5. Drops P(yield condition | drawdown) from ~0.50 to ~0.30 and CQ5 from 27 to ~17; also lowers my CQ4 P90 from 3.1 to ~2.7 and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-C to CQ6-D (bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and inflation is less likely to be accommodated).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE announces a methodology change to, re-basing of, or discontinuation of the BofA MOVE Index, OR publishes a relative-volatility (log/percentage) variant that becomes the quoted benchmark; alternatively, Eurostat formally incorporates owner-occupied housing into the headline HICP with an announced start date inside the window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Pure resolution-mechanics falsification of my lens's premise that the stated indices survive to resolve as written. A MOVE discontinuity makes CQ5 unresolvable-as-stated and I would move it to ~20 (annul risk resolves against YES on strict reading). HICP OOH inclusion adds +0.1-0.3pp to euro core, raising CQ2's OECD leg to ~68% (CQ2 to ~42) and CQ6-C by ~2pp — a probability shift caused entirely by index construction with no change in any underlying price.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'inflation'."}}